# 63. Architecture Decision Records (ADR)

---

# 1. Purpose

Architecture Decision Records (ADRs) capture significant architectural decisions made during the design, implementation and evolution of the Loyalty Platform.

The objective of ADRs is to preserve architectural intent, document trade-offs and provide historical context for future engineering decisions.

Blueprint documents define the architecture.

ADRs explain why important architectural decisions were made.

---

# 2. Scope

An ADR documents only significant architectural decisions.

Examples include:

- Domain Model decisions
- Aggregate ownership
- Data storage strategy
- Event-driven architecture
- API strategy
- Security architecture
- Deployment architecture
- Infrastructure architecture
- Scalability decisions
- External integrations

ADRs do not replace Blueprint documents.

---

# 3. Principles

Every ADR must be:

- permanent;
- immutable;
- version controlled;
- uniquely identified;
- easy to reference;
- technically justified;
- linked to implementation.

ADRs are historical records.

Existing ADRs are never modified after approval.

If a decision changes, a new ADR supersedes the previous one.

---

# 4. ADR Lifecycle

Every ADR follows the lifecycle:

```
Proposed

↓

Under Review

↓

Approved

↓

Implemented

↓

Superseded

↓

Retired
```

Approved ADRs become part of the project's architectural baseline.

---

# 5. ADR Identifier

Every ADR receives a permanent identifier.

Format:

```
ADR-001

ADR-002

ADR-003
```

Identifiers are never reused.

---

# 6. ADR Status

Allowed statuses:

- Proposed
- Accepted
- Implemented
- Superseded
- Deprecated
- Rejected

Only Accepted ADRs may influence implementation.

---

# 7. ADR Template

Every ADR should follow the same structure.

```
ADR Number

Title

Status

Date

Authors

Related Blueprint Documents

Related Engineering Documents

Decision

Context

Problem Statement

Alternatives Considered

Selected Option

Decision Rationale

Consequences

Implementation Impact

Rejected Alternatives

References
```

---

# 8. Decision Quality

Every ADR must explain:

- why the decision exists;
- what alternatives were evaluated;
- why alternatives were rejected;
- expected benefits;
- expected risks;
- implementation impact.

An ADR that records only the final decision without rationale is considered incomplete.

---

# 9. Relationship with Blueprint

Blueprint documents remain the authoritative functional specification.

ADRs provide architectural justification.

If a conflict exists:

Blueprint remains authoritative until a newer Product Decision explicitly changes it.

The corresponding ADR must then be updated by creating a new ADR.

---

# 10. Relationship with Engineering Playbook

Engineering documents define implementation rules.

ADRs define architectural intent.

Engineering decisions must remain compatible with all Accepted ADRs.

