# 13. ADR Review

Every ADR must be reviewed by:

- Solution Architect
- Product Owner (where applicable)
- Security Agent (if security related)
- DevOps Agent (if infrastructure related)

Approval requirements depend on the impact of the decision.

---

# 14. ADR Ownership

Architecture ownership belongs to the Solution Architect.

Implementation Agents may propose new ADRs but may never approve them.

Product decisions remain owned by the Product Owner.

---

# 15. ADR Traceability

Every Accepted ADR should reference:

- Blueprint documents
- Engineering documents
- Modules
- APIs
- Events
- Database objects
- Implementation Tasks

Likewise, implementation tasks should reference the ADRs that justify significant architectural decisions.

---

# 16. Superseding an ADR

An Accepted ADR is never edited.

Instead:

1. create a new ADR;
2. reference the previous ADR;
3. explain why the previous decision changed;
4. describe migration impact;
5. define transition strategy.

Historical ADRs remain available for future reference.

---

# 17. ADR Governance

Architecture changes affecting:

- module boundaries;
- public APIs;
- event contracts;
- aggregate ownership;
- database architecture;
- security model;
- deployment model;
- infrastructure;

must be documented through an ADR before implementation.

Emergency fixes may temporarily bypass this rule but require a retrospective ADR.

---

# 18. Anti-Patterns

The following practices are prohibited:

- undocumented architectural changes;
- changing Blueprint through implementation;
- modifying Approved ADRs;
- implementing architecture "because it feels cleaner";
- introducing framework-driven architecture without business justification.

Architecture exists to support business objectives.

---

# 19. ADR Repository

Recommended structure:

```
docs/

    adr/

        ADR-001-global-customer.md

        ADR-002-membership-model.md

        ADR-003-brand-hierarchy.md

        ...
```

Every ADR should remain individually version controlled.

---

# 20. Definition of ADR Process Ready

The ADR process is considered ready when:

- ADR template is standardized;
- identifier format is defined;
- statuses are defined;
- ownership is defined;
- review process is defined;
- Blueprint relationship is defined;
- Engineering relationship is defined;
- traceability rules are defined;
- superseding rules are defined;
- governance rules are defined.

The ADR process becomes the authoritative mechanism for recording architectural evolution after Blueprint Freeze v1.0.